.TH SCYTALE 1 "2026-09-29" "scytale 0.9" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
scytale \- cryptographic command line tool
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B scytale
.I command
.RI [ verb ]
.I algorithm
.RI [ options ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B scytale list
.RI [ family ]
.RB [ \-\-long ]
.br
.B scytale
.I command
.RI [ verb ]
.B \-\-help
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B scytale
runs the operations of the scytale library from the command line, one
command per module of the library:
.BR hash ,
.BR mac ,
.BR aead ,
.BR cipher ,
.BR kdf ,
.BR key ,
.BR sig ,
.BR kex ,
.BR kem ,
.B pke
and
.BR random .
.PP
The algorithm is the operation. It is the first word after the verb on
every call, never an option and never defaulted, so a script says what
it does and a reader need not know a default:
.B scytale hash sha256
.IR file ,
.B scytale aead encrypt aes\-256\-gcm
and so on. A name is matched without regard to case, and
.B _
and
.B \-
are interchangeable in it. A name that is not known is refused with
the nearest names that are.
.PP
Input is standard input, or the
.I file
named last on the command line. Output is standard output, or the file
named by
.BR \-o .
A file that holds a secret, whether a generated key, a shared secret
or a derived key, is created readable by its owner alone.
.PP
Every option that carries bytes, such as a key, IV, nonce, tag, salt,
signature or label, is written with a prefix that says how to read it
(see
.BR VALUES ).
The algorithm fixes the length of a key, IV or nonce, and a value of
another length is refused with both lengths in the message: nothing is
padded or cut to fit.
.SH VALUES
.TP
.BI hex: digits
Hex, in either case: no
.BR 0x ,
no separators, and an even count of digits.
.TP
.BI file: path
The raw bytes of the file, all of them.
.TP
.BI fd: n
The raw bytes read to the end of descriptor
.IR n ,
for
.B 3<
.I keyfile
in a script.
.TP
.BI env: name
The value of the environment variable
.IR name ,
as hex.
.TP
.BI str: text
The text itself. Allowed for additional data, labels, salts, contexts
and passwords; refused for a key, since a key typed as text is a
password without a key derivation.
.PP
A value without a prefix is an error. A key given as
.B hex:
on the command line is visible to every process on the machine through
.BR ps (1)
and stays in the shell's history, so
.B file:
or
.B fd:
is the better form for one; there is nothing wrong with
.B hex:
for a nonce or a salt, or in a script that already holds the key that
way.
.SH OPTIONS
Every option has a long form, and the long form is what the text
below refers to. The short forms, where a command has them:
.TP
.BR \-k ", " \-\-key
A key value, or for the public-key commands a private key file.
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-public
A public key file; for
.B kex agree
the option is
.BR \-\-peer .
.TP
.BR \-s ", " \-\-signature
A signature value.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-tag
A tag value, for
.BR "mac verify" .
.TP
.BR \-n ", " \-\-nonce
A nonce value.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-length
An output length in bytes.
.TP
.BR \-i ", " \-\-iterations
An iteration count.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-out
The output file, in place of standard output.
.TP
.BR \-\-hex ", " \-\-raw
How bytes are written: hex and a newline, or the bytes themselves.
Each command says below which it does without either. The two
exclude each other.
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
The help for a command, ending with the value syntax and the exit
codes.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
The version.
.SH COMMANDS
.SS list
.B scytale list
.RI [ family ]
.RB [ \-\-long ]
.PP
Prints the algorithm names each command takes, one per line, for a
script to check. With a
.I family
(one of
.BR hash ,
.BR mac ,
.BR aead ,
.BR cipher ,
.BR kdf ,
.BR key ,
.BR sig ,
.BR kex ,
.BR kem ,
.BR pke )
the names alone; without one, every name prefixed by its family. With
.B \-\-long
each line goes on to say what the name takes: the key, nonce, tag and
output lengths, and which options it has.
.SS random
.B scytale random
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.I count
.PP
.I count
bytes from the system-seeded CTR_DRBG. Hex without a flag.
.SS hash
.B scytale hash
.I algorithm
.RI [ "\-l length" ]
.RI [ "\-\-function\-name value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-customization value" ]
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ file... ]
.PP
A digest of each file, or of standard input, as hex followed by two
spaces and the file's name, in the layout of
.BR sha256sum (1);
standard input is named
.BR \- .
With
.B \-\-raw
the digest alone, for one file at most.
.TP
.I algorithm
One of
.BR sha1 ,
.BR sha224 ,
.BR sha256 ,
.BR sha384 ,
.BR sha512 ,
.BR sha512\-224 ,
.BR sha512\-256 ,
.BR sha3\-224 ,
.BR sha3\-256 ,
.BR sha3\-384 ,
.BR sha3\-512 ,
or an extendable-output function,
.BR shake128 ,
.BR shake256 ,
.B cshake128
or
.BR cshake256 .
.TP
.BI \-l,\ \-\-length\  bytes
The output length, required for an extendable-output function and
refused otherwise.
.TP
.BI \-\-function\-name\  value ,\ \-\-customization\  value
The cSHAKE function name and customization string, as
.BR VALUES ;
empty without them, refused for the other hashes.
.SS mac tag, mac verify
.B scytale mac tag
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.RI [ "\-l length" ]
.RI [ "\-\-customization value" ]
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ file ]
.br
.B scytale mac verify
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.BI \-t\  tag
.RI [ "\-l length" ]
.RI [ "\-\-customization value" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
.B tag
writes a tag over the input, hex without a flag.
.B verify
checks the input against
.I tag
instead: nothing is printed, and the exit status says whether it
matched.
.TP
.I algorithm
One of
.BI hmac\- hash
for any hash above
.RB ( hmac\-sha256 ,
.BR hmac\-sha3\-512 ,
\&...),
.BR cmac\-aes\-128 ,
.BR cmac\-aes\-192 ,
.BR cmac\-aes\-256 ,
.BR kmac128 ,
.B kmac256
or
.BR poly1305 .
.TP
.BI \-k,\ \-\-key\  value
The key. HMAC and KMAC take any length; CMAC takes the cipher's, and
Poly1305 32 bytes. A Poly1305 key is for one message only.
.TP
.BI \-l,\ \-\-length\  bytes
The tag length, for KMAC: 32 bytes for
.B kmac128
and 64 for
.B kmac256
without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-customization\  value
The KMAC customization string.
.SS aead encrypt, aead decrypt
.B scytale aead
.RB { encrypt | decrypt }
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.BI \-n\  nonce
.RI [ "\-\-aad value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-tag\-length bytes" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Authenticated encryption. Encryption writes the ciphertext and then the
tag; decryption reads ciphertext and tag as one input, checks the tag,
and writes the plaintext only if it checked. A message that fails
leaves standard output empty and exits with status 1. Encryption with
GCM and ChaCha20-Poly1305 streams; decryption reads the whole input
first, since plaintext handed out before the tag is checked is
plaintext an attacker chose.
.TP
.I algorithm
One of
.BR aes\-128\-gcm ,
.BR aes\-192\-gcm ,
.BR aes\-256\-gcm ,
.BR aes\-128\-gcm\-siv ,
.BR aes\-256\-gcm\-siv ,
.BR aes\-128\-ccm ,
.BR aes\-192\-ccm ,
.BR aes\-256\-ccm ,
.BR aes\-128\-xpn ,
.BR aes\-192\-xpn ,
.BR aes\-256\-xpn ,
.BR chacha20\-poly1305 .
.TP
.BI \-k,\ \-\-key\  value
The key, of the width the name says: 16, 24 or 32 bytes for AES, 32
for ChaCha20-Poly1305.
.TP
.BI \-n,\ \-\-nonce\  value
The nonce. Twelve bytes, except that GCM takes any length, CCM takes 7
to 13, and XPN takes 24: the 12-byte salt followed by the 12-byte
frame. A nonce must never repeat under one key.
.TP
.BI \-\-aad\  value
Additional data, authenticated but not encrypted; the same value must
be given to decrypt. Empty without it.
.TP
.BI \-\-tag\-length\  bytes
The tag length, 16 by default. GCM and XPN take 4 to 16, CCM an even
number from 4 to 16; GCM-SIV and ChaCha20-Poly1305 take 16 only.
.SS cipher encrypt, cipher decrypt
.B scytale cipher
.RB { encrypt | decrypt }
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.RI [ "\-\-iv value" ]
.RI [ "\-n nonce" ]
.RI [ "\-\-counter n" ]
.RI [ "\-\-padding pkcs7|none" ]
.RI [ "\-\-tweak\-key key" ]
.RI [ "\-\-alphabet chars" ]
.RI [ "\-\-tweak value" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
The unauthenticated modes. Nothing here checks that a message is what
was encrypted; a script that needs to know that wants
.BR aead .
These are for a protocol that brings its own MAC, a disk, or a format
to preserve. An option the algorithm does not take is refused, and
the message says which algorithm it is for.
.TP
.I algorithm
.BI aes\- width \- mode
for a
.I width
of 128, 192 or 256 and a
.I mode
of
.BR ecb ,
.BR cbc ,
.BR ctr ,
.BR cfb1 ,
.BR cfb8 ,
.BR cfb128 ,
.B ofb
or
.BR xts ;
.BR chacha20 ;
.BI ff1\-aes\- width
or
.BI ff3\-1\-aes\- width
for the format-preserving modes.
.B kw
and
.B kwp
are under
.BR "cipher wrap" .
.TP
.BI \-k,\ \-\-key\  value
The key, of the cipher's width.
.TP
.BI \-\-iv\  value
One 16-byte block: the IV for CBC, CFB and OFB, the first counter
block for CTR, the tweak for XTS. Required by those modes and refused
by the others.
.TP
.BI \-n,\ \-\-nonce\  value ,\ \-\-counter\  n
The 12-byte nonce and the first block counter (0 without it) for
ChaCha20, which encrypts and decrypts with the one operation.
.TP
.BI \-\-padding\  pkcs7 | none
Required for ECB, CBC and CFB128, which take whole blocks, and
refused by the rest.
.B pkcs7
adds PKCS#7 padding on encryption and removes it on decryption, and
is what
.B openssl enc
and CMS use;
.B none
refuses an input that is not whole blocks. Padding that does not
check on decryption exits with status 1 and prints nothing; a script
must not report that difference to anyone who can submit ciphertexts.
.TP
.BI \-\-tweak\-key\  value
The second key for XTS, of the cipher's width and different from the
first. Required by XTS, refused by the rest.
.TP
.BI \-\-alphabet\  chars ,\ \-\-tweak\  value
For FF1 and FF3-1, which read and write lines of text in the alphabet,
one message per line. The alphabet is the string of characters that
are the numerals, in order; the decimal digits without it. The tweak
is any length for FF1 and exactly 7 bytes for FF3-1; empty for FF1
without it. These modes are not constant time.
.PP
CTR, OFB and CFB take any length and stream; CBC, CFB128 and ECB
stream a block at a time; CFB1, XTS and the format-preserving modes
read the whole input.
.SS cipher wrap, cipher unwrap
.B scytale cipher
.RB { wrap | unwrap }
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  key
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Key wrapping (RFC 3394, RFC 5649): the input is a key, and the output
is it under the wrapping key with an 8-byte check value.
.I algorithm
is
.BI aes\- width \-kw
for a key that is whole 8-byte blocks, two or more, or
.BI aes\- width \-kwp
for a key of any length. A wrapped key that does not check on
unwrapping exits with status 1.
.SS kdf hkdf
.B scytale kdf hkdf
.I hash
.BI \-\-ikm\  value
.RI [ "\-\-salt value" ]
.RI [ "\-\-info value" ...]
.BI \-l\  bytes
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
HKDF (RFC 5869) over
.IR hash ,
any of the plain hashes above: keys from keying material that is
already unguessable, such as a shared secret. The input keying
material may not be given as
.BR str: .
.B \-\-info
may repeat; the values are concatenated in order. Hex without a flag.
.SS kdf pbkdf2
.B scytale kdf pbkdf2
.I hash
.BI \-\-password\  value
.BI \-\-salt\  value
.BI \-i\  iterations
.BI \-l\  bytes
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
PBKDF2 (RFC 8018) over
.IR hash :
a key from a password, which is guessable, at a cost per guess set by
the iteration count. The password and salt may be given as
.BR str: .
Hex without a flag.
.SS key generate
.B scytale key generate
.I algorithm
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
A new private key, as a PEM
.B PRIVATE KEY
block (PKCS#8), to standard output or to
.IR file ,
created readable by its owner alone. The algorithm is one of
.BR ed25519 ,
.BR x25519 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p256 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p384 ,
.BR ecdsa\-p521 ,
.BR ecdh\-p256 ,
.BR ecdh\-p384 ,
.BR ecdh\-p521 ,
.BI rsa\- bits
for any
.I bits
from 1024 to 8192,
.BR ml\-kem\-512 ,
.BR ml\-kem\-768 ,
.BR ml\-kem\-1024 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-44 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-65 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-87 ,
or
.BI slh\-dsa\- hash \- size s
and
.BI slh\-dsa\- hash \- size f
for a
.I hash
of
.B sha2
or
.B shake
and a
.I size
of 128, 192 or 256. Keys on P-256, P-384 and P-521 serve both ECDSA
and ECDH;
an RSA key serves both signatures and encryption.
.SS key public
.B scytale key public
.RI [ file ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
The public half of the private key in
.IR file ,
or standard input, as a PEM
.B PUBLIC KEY
block.
.SS key show
.B scytale key show
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Prints what a key file holds: its algorithm, which half it is, and
for RSA the modulus length.
.PP
Every command that takes a key file reads PEM: the
.B PRIVATE KEY
and
.B PUBLIC KEY
blocks that
.BR openssl (1)
and everything else write, and the bare
.BR "RSA PRIVATE KEY" ,
.B RSA PUBLIC KEY
and
.B EC PRIVATE KEY
forms as well. A command names the scheme it runs and checks the file
holds a key for it; a file that holds another kind of key, or the
wrong half, is refused with a message saying what it holds.
.SS sig sign
.B scytale sig sign
.I scheme
.BI \-k\  private
.RI [ "\-\-context value" ]
.RB [ \-\-raw\-ecdsa ]
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Signs the input with the private key in
.IR private ,
writing the signature as raw bytes without a flag.
.SS sig verify
.B scytale sig verify
.I scheme
.BI \-p\  public
.BI \-s\  signature
.RI [ "\-\-context value" ]
.RB [ \-\-raw\-ecdsa ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
Checks
.I signature
(a value, as
.BR VALUES )
over the input under the public key in
.IR public .
Nothing is printed; the exit status is 0 if it verified and 1 if not.
A private key file is refused here;
.B key public
makes the public one.
.TP
.I scheme
.B ed25519
on an Ed25519 key;
.BI ecdsa\- hash
on a P-256, P-384 or P-521 key, the curve being the key's;
.BI rsa\-pss\- hash
on an RSA or RSA-PSS key, salted with a hash's worth of random bytes;
.BI rsa\-pkcs1\- hash
on an RSA key, deterministic;
.BR ml\-dsa\-44 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-65 ,
.B ml\-dsa\-87
and the twelve
.B slh\-dsa
sets on their own keys.
.I hash
is any of the plain hashes above.
.TP
.BI \-\-context\  value
The context string for Ed25519 (which then signs in the Ed25519ctx
form), ML-DSA and SLH-DSA; up to 255 bytes. Empty without it, which
for Ed25519 is the plain form. Refused by the other schemes.
.TP
.B \-\-raw\-ecdsa
An ECDSA signature as the fixed-width
.I r
followed by
.IR s ,
rather than the DER SEQUENCE that
.B openssl dgst
writes. Ed25519, ML-DSA and SLH-DSA signatures are always the
fixed-width bytes their standards define, and RSA signatures the
modulus length.
.SS kex agree
.B scytale kex agree
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  private
.BI \-p\  peer
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
The shared secret between the private key in
.I private
and the peer's public key in
.IR peer ,
under
.BR x25519 ,
.BR ecdh\-p256 ,
.B ecdh\-p384
or
.BR ecdh\-p521 ;
both keys must be for it. Hex without a flag. A shared secret is
keying material, not a key; derive keys from it with
.BR "kdf hkdf" .
.SS kem encapsulate
.B scytale kem encapsulate
.I algorithm
.BI \-p\  public
.BI \-\-secret\-out\  file
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.PP
A ciphertext for the public key in
.IR public ,
under
.BR ml\-kem\-512 ,
.B ml\-kem\-768
or
.BR ml\-kem\-1024 ,
to standard output or
.BR \-o ,
and the shared secret it carries to
.IR file ,
created readable by its owner alone. Raw bytes without a flag, for
both. The peer recovers the secret from the ciphertext with the
private key.
.SS kem decapsulate
.B scytale kem decapsulate
.I algorithm
.BI \-k\  private
.RB [ \-\-hex | \-\-raw ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
The shared secret a ciphertext carries; hex without a flag. A
ciphertext that was not made for the key yields a secret that matches
nothing, rather than an error, as FIPS 203 requires.
.SS pke encrypt, pke decrypt
.B scytale pke
.RB { encrypt | decrypt }
.I scheme
.RB { "\-p public" | "\-k private" }
.RI [ "\-\-label value" ]
.RI [ "\-o file" ]
.RI [ file ]
.PP
RSA-OAEP under
.BI rsa\-oaep\- hash
for any of the plain hashes: a short message, at most the modulus
length less twice the hash length less two bytes, under an RSA public
key. The label is empty without
.BR \-\-label ,
and must match to decrypt. A ciphertext that does not decrypt exits
with status 1 and says nothing more.
.SH ALGORITHM NAMES
.B scytale list
prints every name, and
.B scytale list
.I family
.B \-\-long
what each takes. Names are lower case with hyphens, and name the
whole construction, key width included:
.BR aes\-256\-gcm ,
.BR hmac\-sha3\-256 ,
.BR rsa\-pss\-sha256 ,
.BR ml\-dsa\-65 .
A key of another width than the name says is refused rather than
taken as a hint.
.SH EXIT STATUS
.TP
.B 0
Success, including a verification that passed.
.TP
.B 1
A tag, signature, padding or check value did not verify: the message
is not what was made under this key.
.TP
.B 2
The request could not be carried out as asked: an unknown name, an
option the algorithm does not take, a value in the wrong form, a key
of the wrong length, a file that is not a key or holds the wrong
kind.
.TP
.B 3
Anything else: a file that would not open, a generator that would not
seed.
.PP
Errors go to standard error as
.BI "scytale " "command verb algorithm" :
followed by what is wrong and what would be right, naming the option
concerned and a length or a name; the bytes of a value are never
shown. When the reader of standard output goes away, the tool stops
quietly with status 0, as the coreutils do.
.SH EXAMPLES
Encrypt a file to a key held in a file, with a header authenticated
alongside it, and decrypt it again:
.PP
.nf
scytale random 32 \-\-raw \-o session.key
scytale random 12 > nonce.hex
scytale aead encrypt aes\-256\-gcm \-k file:session.key \e
    \-n hex:$(cat nonce.hex) \-\-aad str:v1 report.pdf \-o report.sealed
scytale aead decrypt aes\-256\-gcm \-k file:session.key \e
    \-n hex:$(cat nonce.hex) \-\-aad str:v1 report.sealed \-o report.pdf
.fi
.PP
Sign a release with a post-quantum key and check the signature:
.PP
.nf
scytale key generate ml\-dsa\-65 \-o release.pem
scytale key public release.pem \-o release.pub
scytale sig sign ml\-dsa\-65 \-k release.pem release.tar \-o release.sig
scytale sig verify ml\-dsa\-65 \-p release.pub \-s file:release.sig \e
    release.tar
.fi
.PP
Agree a key with a peer and derive session keys from it:
.PP
.nf
scytale key generate x25519 \-o me.pem
scytale key public me.pem \-o me.pub
scytale kex agree x25519 \-k me.pem \-p peer.pub \-\-raw \-o shared.bin
scytale kdf hkdf sha256 \-\-ikm file:shared.bin \-\-salt str:session\-1 \e
    \-\-info str:encrypt \-\-length 32
.fi
.PP
Read a key from a descriptor rather than the command line:
.PP
.nf
scytale mac tag hmac\-sha256 \-k fd:3 message.txt 3< mac.key
.fi
.PP
Decrypt what
.B openssl enc
encrypted:
.PP
.nf
openssl enc \-aes\-128\-cbc \-K $KEY \-iv $IV < plain > cipher
scytale cipher decrypt aes\-128\-cbc \-k hex:$KEY \-\-iv hex:$IV \e
    \-\-padding pkcs7 < cipher
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR openssl (1),
.BR sha256sum (1).
.PP
The library's documentation, at https://docs.rs/scytale, describes
each algorithm, its limits and its security properties; the tool adds
nothing to them.
.SH AUTHOR
Michael Paddon.
